        "id": "ch16_7-1",
        "text": "Using Redirection and Pipes\nRedirection and pipes facilitate the flow of information. A pipe is exactly what it\nsounds like: It directs the output of one program to the input of another program.\nA pipeline may consist of several utilities plumbed together by pipes. At either end\nof this pipeline is, optionally, a redirection.\nAlmost all Linux utilities that require input and output have been plumbed with\nthe following common interfaces: stdin (standard input  \u2014 typically the key-\nboard), stdout (standard output \u2014 typically the monitor), and stderr (standard\nerror \u2014 typically also the monitor). By having a common method to feed input to\na program or read data from the output of a program, you can glue utilities\ntogether into sophisticated solutions.\nEsta secci\u00f3n contiene las siguientes subsecciones\n\u2022 Redirecting command output\n\u2022 Laying pipes\n\u2022 \u201cHelp!\u201d\n\u2022 Clearing the screen\nSi deseas explorar cualquiera de estas subsecciones, puedes pregunt\u00e1rmelo directamente.",
